BOPAlgo_WireSplitter: edges that leave a vertex tangentially to each
other are ordered about it by their directions at one distance from it
(mitcad#235).

The wire splitter orders the edges at each vertex of a face being split
by the angle of each edge's direction there (Angle2D), measured to a point
at a step from the vertex: for a curve other than a line, a step from its
curvature and the vertex's tolerance, used as a step in the curve's own
parameter. On a circle (parametrised by its angle) that is a distance of
about sqrt(2 R tol); on a B-spline it is a distance of that step times the
curve's speed, often a tenth of it or less. Two edges that leave a vertex
tangentially (the section of a thread's crest beside the circle of the
hole it lies in, where a counterbore's end face meets them) differ in
direction by less than their curvature turns them over those steps: the
circle, measured farther out, had turned more than the B-spline beside
it, though the B-spline turns faster. Their order about the vertex came
out the wrong way round, the loops built from it crossed each other, and
the face between the two curves came out with a hole of the other side's
boundary and a negative area; the cut that split it left a body empty.

Edges whose directions at a vertex lie within 0.1 radians of a different
edge's are now measured again, each by the chord to the point where it
first reaches one distance from the vertex, the same for all of them (half
the shortest distance from the vertex to the middle of one of them),
before the existing refinement (RefineAngles). Edges that do not cross
each other keep their order there at any distance: their pieces up to
there are arcs from the vertex to the circle of that radius that do not
cross. Vertices whose edges are too short to tell apart within ten times
the 2d tolerance are left as they were. The same split of a coil's body
by a cut along the bore its thread crosses (the crest's section and the
hole's section leaving their vertices 0.01 to 0.02 radians apart, as
measured) lost faces with a window of 0.01.
